# /etc/rc -- the boot script, run by /etc/init as sh /etc/rc.
#
# THERE IS NO TERMINAL HERE.  runcom in cmd/init/init.c gives this script
# descriptors 0, 1 and 2 on the root DIRECTORY, which can be neither read
# nor written, so every line meant to be seen must redirect to /dev/console
# for itself.
#
# The hash is a real comment: this shell has one, which v7's had not -- see
# cmd/sh/README.md.  So this file no longer has to be written in `:' lines
# whose words are still parsed.

cat /etc/motd >/dev/console

# What the v7 rc did next all wants a program this system has not got yet:
# fsck, mount, rm, date, cron, update, accton.  Each line comes back as its
# command is ported.  There is nothing to mount in any case -- one EC-5052
# drive is the whole of the store, and swap lives on the drums.
